<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guwahati</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guwahati is the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>, in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, on the south bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a>. In ancient times, as Pragjyotishpura, it was a capital of the kingdom of Kamarupa, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakhya-temple">Kamakhya</a> temple on Nilachal hill is a major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> pilgrimage site. Contested between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahom-people">Ahoms</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> in the 17th century, it is now the main commercial centre of the region, with the state capital at Dispur within it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guwahati.jpg" alt="Guwahati" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rituraj Bhuyan from Guwahati, India · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guwahati_citysky.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Guwahati lies between the south bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra River</a> and the foothills of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shillong">Shillong</a> plateau, with several hills inside the city limits. The Bharalu River, a tributary of the Brahmaputra, flows through the centre, and the freshwater lake of Dipor Bil to the south-west acts as a natural stormwater reservoir. The city is known as the gateway to North East India. Its name comes from the Assamese guwa, areca nut, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> guvaka, and hati, rows: the rows of areca nut trees.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The ancient cities of Pragjyotishpura and Durjaya, the latter now North Guwahati, were capitals of the ancient state of Kamarupa. Legend ascribes the founding of Pragjyotisha to the asura king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narakasura">Naraka</a>, who appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> and is said in later texts to have been slain by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> and Satyabhama. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakhya-temple">Kamakhya</a> temple on Nilachal hill, an important seat of Tantric and Vajrayana Buddhism, and the Navagraha temple on Chitrachal hill lie within the city, which is called the City of Temples.</p><p>The Koch king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parikshit">Parikshit</a> had his capital at Pragjyotishpur during his conflicts with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>. The area came under Mughal occupation in 1633-59, 1662-69 and 1679-81, and its vestiges were removed after the Battle of Itakhuli. Pandu, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a> in the west of the city, served as the chief military base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahom-people">Ahoms</a> against external invasions, protected by fortifications called gorh. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> was ceded to the British in 1826, Guwahati became the seat of British administration there, until the headquarters moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shillong">Shillong</a> in 1874. The Gauhati High Court was established in 1948, and in 1972 the government of Assam decided to move its capital from Shillong to Dispur, where the Assembly first sat on 16 March 1973.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Guwahati is the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> and of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, with 962,334 people at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Dispur, the state capital, lies within it. The core area has an old city with Pan Bazaar, Paltan Bazaar, Fancy Bazaar and Uzan Bazaar. Pandu port is the largest river port in Assam. The city is a centre of education, with the Indian Institute of Technology Guwahati, Cotton University and Gauhati University among its institutions. The Gauhati High Court serves Assam, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagaland">Nagaland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mizoram">Mizoram</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arunachal-pradesh">Arunachal Pradesh</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Guwahati” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Ahoms retook <b>Guwahati</b> in 1667, and in 1671 the general Lachit Borphukan defeated a Mughal force under Raja Ram Singh I of Amber at the Battle of Saraighat, fought largely on the river.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ahom-kingdom">Ahom kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…as early literature in each: Luipa was from Kamarupa, and Sarahapa is said to have come from Rani near present-day <b>Guwahati</b>; Rahul Sankrityayan held that most of the poets were from Bihar and wrote an early Magahi, and K. P. Jayaswal took the language for an early Maithili.</blockquote><a href="/songs/charyapada">Charyapada</a></li><li><blockquote>In January 1946 at <b>Guwahati</b>, Gandhi urged that the new greeting &quot;Jai Hind&quot; should not replace Vande Mataram.</blockquote><a href="/songs/vande-mataram">Vande Mataram</a></li><li><blockquote>Koch Hajo was a Hindu kingdom whose rulers patronised the Hayagriva Madhava temple at Hajo and the Kamakhya temple near <b>Guwahati</b>, which the Koch had rebuilt.</blockquote><a href="/state/koch-hajo">Koch Hajo</a></li></ul></section>
